{"timestamp":"2026-09-30T08:05:01.491Z","level":"info","event":"provider.together.response","fields":{"provider":"together","endpoint":"/chat/completions","response":{"httpStatus":200,"contentType":"text/event-stream","providerRequestId":"a133ba58-aws_ec1","model":"moonshotai/Kimi-K3","text":"{\"schemaVersion\":\"one-shot-brief-v1\",\"subject\":\"Structural engineering basics\",\"lessonTitle\":\"How a Truss Bridge Carries Load\",\"coverage\":[{\"point\":\"A truss is a frame of triangles, and triangles stay rigid because they cannot change shape without a side changing length, unlike a rectangle that can lean into a parallelogram\",\"sectionIds\":[\"7000bc32-3148-77e3-9ff8-c20dcee039fd\"]},{\"point\":\"Every truss member carries force along its length, either tension pulling it or compression pushing it\",\"sectionIds\":[\"b1fad4ef-9193-7d27-bd84-fa13a62855d9\"]},{\"point\":\"In a simple bridge truss the top chord is in compression, the bottom chord is in tension, and diagonals alternate between the two\",\"sectionIds\":[\"b1fad4ef-9193-7d27-bd84-fa13a62855d9\"]},{\"point\":\"The load path runs from the vehicle's weight on the deck, to the truss joints, from member to member toward the ends\",\"sectionIds\":[\"b62b6d9a-678b-71d7-8310-bf4e632a9495\"]},{\"point\":\"Supports at each end push up on the truss and pass the load into the ground, completing the load path\",\"sectionIds\":[\"b62b6d9a-678b-71d7-8310-bf4e632a9495\"]}],\"notCovered\":[\"Why trusses use less material than solid beams (section a16aed0f) — beyond the requested scope\",\"How trusses fail, including buckling and joint design (section 6e4f872b) — beyond the requested scope\"],\"plannedSceneCount\":6,\"stylePackId\":\"systems\",\"stylePackReason\":\"Explaining a load path and member forces is cause-and-effect; fine connectors and precise diagrams suit animated force arrows along a truss.\",\"soundBed\":\"quiet-pulse\",\"soundBedReason\":\"Focused, calm bed supports a steady walkthrough of forces for intermediate adult learners without distraction.\"}","textChunks":["{\"schemaVersion\":\"one-shot-brief-v1\",\"subject\":\"Structural engineering basics\",\"lessonTitle\":\"How a Truss Bridge Carries Load\",\"coverage\":[{\"point\":\"A truss is a frame of triangles, and triangles stay rigid because they cannot change shape without a side changing length, unlike a rectangle that can lean into a parallelogram\",\"sectionIds\":[\"7000bc32-3148-77e3-9ff8-c20dcee039fd\"]},{\"point\":\"Every truss member carries force along its length, either tension pulling it or compression pushing it\",\"sectionIds\":[\"b1fad4ef-9193-7d27-bd84-fa13a62855d9\"]},{\"point\":\"In a simple bridge truss the top chord is in compression, the bottom chord is in tension, and diagonals alternate between the two\",\"sectionIds\":[\"b1fad4ef-9193-7d27-bd84-fa13a62855d9\"]},{\"point\":\"The load path runs from the vehicle's weight on the deck, to the truss joints, from member to member toward the ends\",\"sectionIds\":[\"b62b6d9a-678b-71d7-8310-bf4e632a9495\"]},{\"point\":\"Supports at each end push up on the truss and pass the load into the ground, completing the load path\",\"sectionIds\":[\"b62b6d9a-678b-71d7-8310-bf4e632a9495\"]}],\"notCovered\":[\"Why trusses use less material than solid beams (section a16aed0f) — beyond the requested scope\",\"How trusses fail, including buckling and joint design (section 6e4f872b) — beyond the requested scope\"],\"plannedSceneCount\":6,\"stylePackId\":\"systems\",\"stylePackReason\":\"Explaining a load path and member forces is cause-and-effect; fine connectors and precise diagrams suit animated force arrows along a truss.\",\"soundBed\":\"quiet-pulse\",\"soundBedReason\":\"Focused, calm bed supports a steady walkthrough of forces for intermediate adult learners without distraction.\"}"],"textChunksTruncated":false,"textCharacters":1677,"reasoningCharacters":0,"finishReason":"stop","inputTokens":"[REDACTED]","outputTokens":"[REDACTED]"},"retries":0,"latencyMs":